Mild Graves' Ophthalmopathy
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Minimal proptosis, no visual impairment, lid retraction only

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-029
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe gritty eye pain, tearing, and photophobia requiring immediate exclusion of a corneal abrasion or acute ulcer.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute unilateral eye pain and severe foreign body sensation. Immediate clinical review was strictly necessary to rule out an acute corneal ulceration or sight-threatening infection requiring emergency ophthalmic intervention prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: It feels like there is crushed glass under my eyelids, they are burning uncontrollably, and the light in the room is agonizing.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a history of gritty eyes and mild photophobia associated with controlled autoimmune thyroid disease. Tonight, the patient was reading in front of a fan when the left eye suddenly developed an intense, stinging foreign body sensation, accompanied by profuse reflex tearing and marked photophobia. The patient is unable to keep the eye open and fears severe structural damage or impending blindness.
Functional Status: Independent, but visually limited tonight due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, in acute discomfort, wearing sunglasses indoors and holding a tissue to the left eye.
Physical Exam: Ophthalmic examination reveals bilateral minimal proptosis, no visual impairment, lid retraction only. The left eye shows mild conjunctival injection but no ciliary flush. Fluorescein staining reveals diffuse superficial punctate epitheliopathy across the lower third of the cornea, but strictly no large focal uptake, dendritic ulcers, or foreign bodies. Pupils are equal and reactive. Visual acuity is intact when the eye is manually held open.
Environmental Observations: Overhead fan was directly blowing air onto the patient's face; instructed to turn off.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient occasionally uses over-the-counter artificial tears but ran out yesterday.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Exposure Keratopathy secondary to Mild Graves' Ophthalmopathy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Corneal Ulcer/Infectious Keratitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a dense, focal white corneal infiltrate or localized severe fluorescein uptake, and lack of purulent discharge.

Corneal Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Eversion of the upper and lower eyelids revealing no retained particulate matter, and no linear tracking scratches on fluorescein stain.

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal palpated intraocular pressure, reactive pupils, and absence of severe deep aching pain or nausea.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe ocular surface pain and photophobia, requiring immediate exclusion of a sight-threatening corneal ulcer or retained foreign body. Triage correctly mandated urgent evaluation. Bedside assessment with fluorescein staining definitively ruled out a focal structural ulcer or foreign object, confirming severe exposure keratitis secondary to underlying Graves' lid retraction, exacerbated by environmental airflow. This timely intervention safely averted an unnecessary midnight presentation to the emergency department, provided immediate symptomatic relief, and protected the cornea from further exposure damage overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single drop of topical anesthetic for thorough examination, which immediately abolished the pain. Instilled thick lubricating eye ointment (e.g., paraffin-based) post-exam.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a generous ribbon of the thick eye ointment exactly 10 minutes before going to sleep and gently tape the eyelid horizontally closed with surgical tape. By artificially sealing the eye shut with lubrication, you completely prevent the draft from drying the cornea, ensuring you wake up pain-free.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine optometry or GP review within 3-5 days to optimize daily lubrication regimens.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you experience a sudden, profound drop in your vision, if the eye begins producing thick yellow pus, or if deep, severe pain returns and radiates to your brow or jaw.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Because the eyelids rest slightly higher due to the thyroid condition, the bottom of the eye dries out rapidly, causing severe stinging. It is entirely manageable with aggressive lubrication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if the light sensitivity becomes completely intolerable despite using the thick ointment, or if your pupil looks irregular in shape.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain is from thousands of microscopic dry spots on the surface of the eye, like severely chapped lips. There is no scratch or infection. The thick ointment acts as a protective shield.
Actionable Steps: Never sleep with a fan blowing directly on your face. Use thick lubricating gel drops at least four times a day, not just when the eye hurts.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Severe dry eye/exposure keratitis (related to thyroid). No corneal ulcers or scratches found. Thick lubricating ointment applied. See GP/Optometrist in 3 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Use thick eye ointment before bed and avoid fans blowing on your face. Go to the ED if you suffer a sudden loss of vision or the eye produces thick yellow pus.
